The default block (lines 59–80) covers the case where the option is *unset* (default true). The user wants to explicitly confirm that `eraseOnClick: true` clears on click. Adding a describe block with the explicit `{ drawable: { eraseOnClick: true } }` setting, mirroring the three click types — this pins down the explicit true path independently from the default.

Insert after line 80 (end of the default block), before the `eraseOnClick = false` block. Use `PUT >80:` to insert after the `});` on line 80... no wait, the order within the file: default block, then false block. Should I put the explicit true block right after the default block (to keep the "clear" behavior grouped together), or right before the false block? Either works. I'll insert it after line 80.

Content:

```ts
describe('drawable.eraseOnClick = true (explicit)', () => {
  const config: Config = { drawable: { eraseOnClick: true } };

  test('clicking an empty square erases all shapes', () => {
    const { chess, boardEl } = setup(config);
    withArrow(chess);
    click(boardEl, 'd4');
    expect(chess.state.drawable.shapes).toHaveLength(0);
  });

  test('clicking an immovable piece erases all shapes', () => {
    const { chess, boardEl } = setup(config);
    withArrow(chess);
    click(boardEl, 'd7');
    expect(chess.state.drawable.shapes).toHaveLength(0);
  });

  test('clicking a movable piece erases all shapes', () => {
    const { chess, boardEl } = setup(config);
    withArrow(chess);
    click(boardEl, 'e2');
    expect(chess.state.drawable.shapes).toHaveLength(0);
  });
});
```

Note: this file uses CRLF line endings (I converted it with sed). The edit tool should handle that; the body lines get written as-is... hmm, will the edit tool write my lines with LF into a CRLF file, creating mixed line endings? The previous edits to this file (line 1, line 30) were done *before* the CRLF conversion — so the current file: my earlier edited lines are LF, the rest are CRLF? Wait, no — the conversion to CRLF was done after all edits, via sed 's/$/\r/'. That added \r to every line, including the lines I had previously edited (since at that point they had no \r). So the file is now uniformly CRLF.

Now if I insert new lines with the edit tool, those lines will be LF. That'll be a trivial mix. To keep it uniform, I can just run sed 's/$/\r/' again after editing — but that would double up \r on lines that already have it! `sed 's/$/\r/'` on a line ending in \r\n: the line content ends with \r, and sed appends another \r → \r\r\n. No good.

Options: use a targeted sed that only appends \r to lines not ending in \r: `sed -i '/\r$/!s/$/\r/'`. That's safe — it only converts lines that are missing \r.

Then verify with `file` and re-run the tests.

